## 9. Speaking up

You can raise a concern with:

- your manager, or their manager;

- the People team (Marco Bianchi, Head of People);

- Compliance (Sofia Lindqvist);

- Helen Marsh, General Counsel;

- the independent whistleblowing line, by phone or online, anonymously if you prefer;

- the FCA directly, if you prefer.

Kestrel will not tolerate anyone being treated badly for raising a concern in good faith, even if it turns out to be mistaken.

## Interns

Interns follow the same list, except:

- they have no access to Restricted folders, even if their team does, unless their manager asks Marco Bianchi in writing;

- they do not join on-call rotas;

- their end-of-placement review replaces the month 3 probation review.

## 6. What changed in version 3.1

The FCA's new safeguarding rules apply from 7 May 2026. Because of them:

- the monthly safeguarding return to the FCA was added;

- the resolution pack is now checked every month instead of every year;

- the 15:00 statement file check, started after breach SG-2026-01 in March, is now part of the written procedure.

## 1. Before the start date

The manager emails it@harbourhealth.example at least **5 working days** before the start date with:

- full name, role, base clinic and start date;

- whether they need a laptop or will use the shared clinic PCs only;

- which shared mailboxes and folders they need (for example the Operations folder for duty managers);

- whether they will be a rota'd member of staff.

IT then:

1. creates the Microsoft 365 account (format firstname.lastname@harbourhealth.example);

2. adds the account to the right groups: everyone joins Harbour, and duty managers join Operations;

3. prepares a laptop if one is needed, enrolled in device management and encrypted;

4. books the new starter on the next onboarding session.

## 2. Onboarding session

Sam Patel runs the onboarding session every **Tuesday at 09:30** at Harbourside, in the training room on the first floor. It takes about 1 hour. New starters bring their phone.

In the session they:

- sign in for the first time and set a password;

- set up multi-factor authentication with the Microsoft Authenticator app;

- learn how to log on and off the shared clinic PCs;

- learn where the policies are (the Policies folder) and how to find the rota;

- learn how to report a suspicious email.

Anyone who cannot attend in their first week must have the session within 10 working days of starting. Until then, they can use a shared clinic PC only with a duty manager signed in alongside them.

## 4. New suppliers

To add a supplier to the approved list, send George Evans:

- the supplier's company details and VAT number;

- their bank details on headed paper, confirmed by phone to a number we already hold or find independently, never one given in the same email;

- proof of insurance where they work on our sites;

- a completed data protection questionnaire if they will handle any staff personal data.

New suppliers are approved within 5 working days. Changes to a supplier's bank details always need a phone check by Finance before the next payment.

## 2. Minimum cover

While a clinic is open it must have at least:

- 1 duty manager on site;

- 2 reception staff (1 on Saturdays);

- 1 member of staff trained in fire marshalling.

If cover falls below this, the duty manager tells Isla McGregor straight away. Out of hours, they call the operations on-call phone. If cover cannot be found within 2 hours, the Operations Director decides whether to reduce the clinic's hours.
